LORD, kept track of iniquities, then who, O Lord, could stand?","verse":3},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"I wait for the LORD; my soul does wait, and in His word I put my hope.","verse":5},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"O Israel, put your hope in the LORD, for with the LORD is loving devotion, and with Him is redemption in abundance.","verse":7}],"id":"Lord","label":"LORD","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","scope":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","senseReview":"Only literal uppercase LORD is admitted globally. Source Lord article explicitly covers God as Lord; lowercase human lord and sentence-title Lord are not inferred from this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord","term":"LORD"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Lord","sourceTitle":"Lord"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"mercy","senseEvidence":{"basis":"source_verse_citation","originalCitations":[{"anchorIndex":29,"href":"?bref=Ps.130.1-8","rawReference":"Ps.130.1-8"}],"ruleId":"Mercy::Mercy::source_verse_citation","sourceHeadwordId":"Mercy"},"text":"O Lord, hear my voice; let Your ears be attentive to my plea for mercy.","verse":2}],"id":"Mercy","label":"mercy","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Mercy::Mercy::source_verse_citation","scope":"source_verse_citation","senseReview":"Reviewed original headword and source sense. This substantive word is admitted only where the original article explicitly cites the exact occurrence verse; ambiguous homographs and known context mismatches are separately excluded. This is selective contextual evidence, not an automatic rule for every same-spelling word.","sourceHeadwordId":"Mercy","term":"Mercy"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Mercy","sourceTitle":"Mercy"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Ps.120.1-134.3","r":"Ps.120.1-134.3","rawR":"Ps.120.1-134.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.120.1-134.3","value":"Ps.120.1-134.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"Pss 120–134"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," These short, appealing songs were sung during pilgrims’ journeys to Jerusalem for the national festivals three times per year (",{"href":"?bref=Deut.16.16","r":"Deut.16.16","rawR":"Deut.16.16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.16.16","value":"Deut.16.16"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 16:16"},"). The title of these psalms includes the Hebrew word for stairs, which some take to mean “ascending to Jerusalem.” These psalms (sometimes called the Songs of Ascents) take the reader on a pilgrimage to Jerusalem to praise God for his goodness to Israel throughout history."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Ps.120.1-134.3","refs":[{"book":"PSA","endChapter":134,"endVerse":3,"startChapter":120,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Ps.120.1-134.3","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"PSA","sourceChapterCoverage":[120,121,122,123,124,125,126,127,128,129,130,131,132,133,134],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"804bdc0d4264fc96adf7bf16357af3c33f1b33647dde7b70863e764ccb4e0941","sourceItemName":"Ps.120.1-134.3","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7297,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"PSA","canonicalOSIS":"Ps","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Ps"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Ps.120.1-134.3","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Ps.120.1-134.3","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Ps.130.1-8","r":"Ps.130.1-8","rawR":"Ps.130.1-8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.130.1-8","value":"Ps.130.1-8"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 130"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Beginning with himself as a representative of the community, the psalmist cries for God’s mercy (",{"href":"?bref=Ps.130.1-3","r":"Ps.130.1-3","rawR":"Ps.130.1-3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.130.1-3","value":"Ps.130.1-3"}],"t":"ref","v":"130:1-3"},") out of deep distress. He places his hope in the Lord (",{"href":"?bref=Ps.130.4-6","r":"Ps.130.4-6","rawR":"Ps.130.4-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.130.4-6","value":"Ps.130.4-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"130:4-6"},"), knowing that the Lord will rescue when and as he chooses. Based on this confidence, the psalmist invites Israel to wait for the Lord (",{"href":"?bref=Ps.130.7-8","r":"Ps.130.7-8","rawR":"Ps.130.7-8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.130.7-8","value":"Ps.130.7-8"}],"t":"ref","v":"130:7-8"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Ps.130.1-8","refs":[{"book":"PSA","endChapter":130,"endVerse":8,"startChapter":130,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Ps.130.1-8","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"PSA","sourceChapterCoverage":[130],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"26631c05edb11348a03b1d07afc4a821c55456839a5c62835bfe232de6726123","sourceItemName":"Ps.130.1-8","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7340,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"PSA","canonicalOSIS":"Ps","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Ps"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Ps.130.1-8","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Ps.130.1-8","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAsso